          %h2#idea Idea
          %p
            Quantum information refers to data that can be physically stored in a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics"} quantum system
          %p
            Quantum information theory is the study of how such information can be encoded, measured, and manipulated. A notable sub-field is
            = succeed "," do
              %em quantum computation
          %p
            Categorical quantum information refers to a program in which the cogent aspects of
            = succeed "-based" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hilbert+space"} Hilbert space
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+categories"} symmetric monoidal categories
          %h2#quantum_protocols_and_algorithms Quantum protocols and algorithms
          %p
            Brief synopsis of teleportation,
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/entanglement"} entanglement
            swapping, BB84, E91, Deutsch-Jozsa, Shor should go here…
          %h2#categorytheoretic_formulation Category-theoretic formulation
          %p
            There is a formulation of (aspects of)
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+mechanics+in+terms+of+dagger-compact+categories"} quantum mechanics in terms of dagger-compact categories
          %p
            The linear adjoint
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo †
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^\dagger
            gives Hilbert spaces the structure of a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%E2%80%A0-category"} †-category
            = succeed "," do
              %strong †-symmetric monoidal category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/symmetric+monoidal+category"} symmetric monoidal category
            equipped with a symmetric monoidal functor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo †
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (-)^\dagger
            from
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Hilb
                    %mi op
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hilb^{op}
            to
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Hilb
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Hilb
            %em finite dimensional
            Hilbert spaces forms a
            = succeed "," do
              %strong
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dagger-compact+category"} †-compact closed category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/compact+closed+category"} compact closed category
            such that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi A
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A_*
            \:=
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo *
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo †
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi A
                    %mo †
                  %msup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo *
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (A^*)^\dagger = (A^\dagger)^*
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi η
                      %mi A
                    %msup
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo †
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi A
                          %mo *
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\eta_A)^\dagger = \epsilon_{A^*}
          %h3#graphical_notation Graphical notation
          %p
            Graphical notation via
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Penrose+notation"} Penrose notation
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/string+diagrams"} string diagrams
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor+networks"} tensor networks
          %p
            Morphisms in a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monoidal+category"} monoidal category
            (and 2-categories in general) are inherently two dimensional, where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ∘
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \circ
            is
            %em vertical
            composition and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ⊗
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \otimes
            is
            %em horizontal
            composition. These satisfy an
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/interchange+law"} interchange law
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∘
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ⊗
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi f
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ∘
                  %msub
                    %mi g
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (f_1 \otimes f_2) \circ (g_1 \otimes g_2) = (f_1 \circ g_1) \otimes (f_2 \circ g_2)
          %p So, if we think of these four morphisms as occupying a spot in 2 dimensional space:
          .query
            %p
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Aleks+Kissinger"}> Aleks Kissinger
              \: TODO: figure
          %p
            we realize that the bracketing from above is essentially meaningless syntax. This notion is the guiding concept for the graphical notation of monoidal categories, or string diagrams. In this notation, we represent objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                  %mo ,
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A,B
            as directed strings and arrows
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi f
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                  %mo →
                  %mi B
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} f : A \rightarrow B
            as boxes.
          %div{:style => "text-align:center"}
